    model: 'If the impressions sat too long before pouring, the alginate lost water through syneresis and shrank, or absorbed water through imbibition and swelled if left in water, so the models were distorted and the tray did not fit. Removing the tray slowly or rocking it can also tear or distort the impression, and an incorrect water-to-powder ratio or water temperature changes setting and accuracy. Bubbles in the models come from mixing stone without enough spatulation, not using a vibrator, pouring too much material at once or pouring from several directions so air is trapped in the teeth, or from an incorrect water-to-powder ratio. The correct procedure is to choose a tray that fits, measure water and powder according to the manufacturer, spatulate to a smooth creamy mix, load the tray and seat it from back to front, hold it still until set, remove it with a quick snap, rinse and disinfect it and keep it in a sealed moist bag, then pour it promptly. Type III dental stone is mixed with measured water, vibrated and poured slowly from one side, then separated after setting and trimmed. Finally, a plastic sheet is heated in a vacuum-former, pulled down over the model, cooled, trimmed just short of the gingiva and tried in the patient’s mouth.',
