  takeaways: ['Local anesthetics block sodium channels so pain impulses cannot travel.', 'Amides (lidocaine, articaine, mepivacaine, prilocaine, bupivacaine) are metabolized in the liver; esters (benzocaine) cause more allergies.', 'Epinephrine prolongs anesthesia, reduces bleeding and lowers toxicity. 2% = 20 mg/mL; one 1.8 mL cartridge of 2% lidocaine = 36 mg.', 'IANB numbs mandibular teeth to the midline, lower lip and chin; PSA, MSA, ASA, greater palatine and nasopalatine cover the maxilla. Aspirate before injecting.', 'Nitrous oxide: scavenge, then 100% oxygen 3–5 minutes. NSAIDs first-line for dental pain; prophylaxis with amoxicillin only for highest-risk cardiac patients.'],
